%S A400812 0,0,0,28,372,3604,31268,258464,2086984,16651528,132032296,1043449220
%N A400812 Number of star triples in the level-n supertile of the Spectre tiling.
%C A400812 Introduced in 2023 by Smith, Myers, Kaplan and Goodman-Strauss, the Spectre monotile is a chiral aperiodic monotile: a single tile that tiles the plane only aperiodically, without using its mirror image. The level-n supertile is obtained from a single Spectre tile by n inflation steps of the S8/S9 substitution (see Smith et al., Figure 2.2) and contains A057080(n) tiles.
%C A400812 A star triple is a set of three Spectre tiles related by a rotation through 120 degrees about a common point of their boundaries. Up to orientation, there are exactly two such configurations, the major and the minor star triple (A400813, A400814), which differ in their circumradius. Star triples first appear at inflation level 3 and are the local 3-fold symmetry centers of the tiling (see also Imperor-Clerc, Sadoc and Tatham).
%C A400812 Adjacent star triples form clusters of five families (sizes A397115-A397119), which are chains of three kinds of megaobjects (sizes A397120, A400810, A400811). The numbers of star triples in the individual cluster families (A400815-A400819) and in the individual kinds of megaobjects (A400820-A400822) all contain a component that grows with 5.6744..., the largest root of x^3 - 3*x^2 - 15*x - 1. This component cancels exactly in the sum over the five cluster families and in the sum over the three kinds of megaobjects, so that a(n) contains only the growth rates 4+sqrt(15) and 2+sqrt(5). Tables, growth rules and figures are given in Horvat, "Star triples in the Spectre tiling: tables" (see Links).
%C A400812 The recurrence and formulas were derived from the growth rules of the cluster families and megaobjects, which were found empirically from direct counts in the supertiles of levels 3 to 7; they are not proved.
%C A400812 a(0)-a(9) were confirmed by direct counts in the level-n supertiles built exactly from the S8/S9 substitution, and a(10)-a(11) by adding to the counts in the child supertiles the star triples that cross their seams. - _Alper Ferudun_, Oct 23 2026
